      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡a=0.72,$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14269832/assets/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡a \\\\  & =-0.72.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
